Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.309 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  nurses have been on the front lines of  the pandemic since the very beginning  we're working in a state of very high  stress  the psychological tension has gone  through the roof  many paid for their service with their  lives  the world health organization estimates  that 115  000 nurses worldwide have died in  connection with a coronavirus infection  is the nurse's hard work being given the  appreciation it deserves  many are still waiting for the financial  applause  [Applause]  and they are demanding better working  conditions  how can it be that the heroes of the  pandemic are still struggling  for recognition welcome to your covet 19  special i'm chris coburn berlin  despite their heroic efforts healthcare  workers around the world are now  learning  that they are subject to a rather  unforgiving system  take spain where more than 80 doctors  and nurses themselves  fell victim to the coronavirus the  government promised  to improve working conditions but  instead with infection rates declining  thousands of healthcare workers have  already been laid off  or soon will be dw's young philip  schultz reports from valencia  in eastern spain laura  almodever has been told her services are  no longer required here on the kovid 19  ward but she still has her hands full  many of her colleagues at the valencia  clinic  are gone in the surrounding region alone  hundreds of nurses were let go overnight  many of her co-workers were informed  that they'd been laid off  in a text message  i think it's shocking to find out your  employment has been terminated like this  it shows zero respect for the people who  gave everything during the pandemic  a standing ovation for health workers  the heroes of the pandemic  when spain was particularly hard hit by  coronavirus in march 2020  thousands of nurses were hired at short  notice many  fell ill some even died of the virus  but now the covid19 19 numbers are  falling across the country  these nurses are no longer needed more  than 1 000  nurses have been laid off here in the  valencia region in the past few weeks  alone  for thousands more their professional  future is uncertain  within a few months they seem to have  gone from heroes of the pandemic  to redundant personnel the layoffs have  led to furious protests here  most of those who have been sacked are  afraid to speak to us  on camera only one person is willing to  speak anonymously  by telephone he tells us that he and his  colleagues feel abandoned  when the virus happened we were short of  everything we needed to fight it  we were brutally overloaded with work we  couldn't even take care of our own  health  and that had extreme effects on our  private and family life  now it all seems like exploitation to me  in recent years spain has made massive  cuts to its public health system  more than a third of medical staff only  have short-term contracts  and many clinics have been privatized  during the pandemic the government  promised improvements  but instead the situation has worsened  and yet those in charge  haven't changed their tune  we are currently making great efforts to  create more permanent positions and to  ensure that fewer people have to work in  unsafe conditions  we hope that the situation will improve  in 2022.  for nurse laura almodever and her fired  colleagues  there could be some good news at least  for now  many of them will be reinstated over the  summer to fill in for others  who've gone on vacation  let's get more on this with howard  catton he's the ceo of the international  council of nurses icn and he joins me  from geneva in switzerland welcome back  to our show howard  zero respect for the people who gave  everything during the pandemic as one  nurse called the sacking of healthcare  workers in the report we just seen  do you agree i think this is a  shameful way to treat nurses and health  care workers  these are the people who have put  themselves ahead of everybody  else who've gone to work knowing that  there's a greater  risk that they might become infected  haven't had the personal protective  equipment that they need  have often separated themselves from  their family so as not to present a risk  to them who have stepped into the shoes  of  relatives they are not disposable  they are indispensable they are the  heart of our  health care systems and we're seeing  around the world  uh nurses who are angry at this  lack of recognition and respect and an  increase in strikes in europe in  africa and in the pacific region as well  on that note um  healthcare workers do have a huge  leverage particularly now on the  pandemic  if protests grew if more nurses went on  strike  that would create enormous pressure for  their cause wouldn't it  we went into this pandemic six million  nurses  short globally we've got aging nursing  workforce in many  countries we've got nurses who have left  because of  burnout because of anxiety we don't know  the impact of long coved the last thing  we can afford to do  is to lose our nurses our hospitals our  clinics  without staff without nurses they're  just empty buildings it's the people  that matter  and we should be investing and in them  and protecting them  now the pandemic is at a different stage  in different parts of the world  do you see situations improving for  healthcare workers in areas  with high infection rates because of the  lessons learned  earlier elsewhere  we are seeing many places where  nurses are taking on increasing  leadership positions they're at the  forefront of the public health messages  organizing systems of care as as well  and the situation is improving but  we are also seeing areas where uh the  virus is resurgent and there is a huge  issue at the moment  with the inequity of access to vaccines  for nurses and healthcare workers there  are nurses and health workers in some  parts of the world  still waiting to be vaccinated whilst  younger people are being jabbed in other  countries we all know we're not going to  win this  race against the virus on our own as  nations it is a global race and we need  to see  much more sharing and solidarity and  cooperation around the world  in an icn report published earlier this  year it says uh  studies across regions suggest that the  pandemic  has exacerbated the stress and anxiety  felt by nurses and other health care  workers what do these people suffer from  in particular  we have seen huge physical and  psychological impacts  on nursing and healthcare staff physical  exhaustion insomnia  burnout and stress but also  the emotional burden of being away from  your family  looking after people more people who are  seriously ill  who are fearful more people who are  dying  on top of that we have also seen  examples of  discrimination and violence against  nurses  people who may be ignorant or fearful  but you know nurses who've not been able  to renew their tenancy because their  landlord is worried that they're  spreading the virus nurses having  difficulty accessing  child care for their children as well  and nurses being subject to  the kovid deniers who are saying this  whole thing is a hoax  we we've called this a mass  traumatization of the nursing workforce  and that's why we need to be  focusing on the recovery of our nurses  in our healthcare  workforce right now howard catton of the  international council of nurses  thank you for your thoughts thank you  getting the right jab has for many  people been the most promising  way to protect themselves against a  severe coronavirus infection  vaccines have been subject of many of  your questions  here's one and our science correspondent  derek williams  every country has a different strategy  to prioritize vaccines  what does the science say about how to  best allocate them  we're still in the middle of the biggest  vaccine drive  in history so evaluating the moving data  on this topic  is really a pretty tall order to be  honest  i don't even know if looking back in the  years to come  if we'll be able to say anything with  certainty  about whether countries might have  prioritized  the right groups for vaccine allocation  in the right order that's because  demographics are so different in  different countries that  that making blanket statements about  what works best  can i think provide at best just  rough guidelines there are just too many  variables  the big problem is that allocating  vaccines means  making choices that are at odds with  each other  the most obvious of those choices is is  whether to cut  down on the death and suffering caused  by the disease  as quickly as possible by vaccinating  the most vulnerable first  or to instead try to vaccinate those  who are most exposed so so those who  will catch the disease most often  and hence really drive further  transmission  countries with aging populations like  many  here in europe went for the first option  they decided to  prioritize the elderly the group that's  most at risk  if they contract covet 19 and and that  choice seems to be working at preventing  death and suffering um a british health  care authority for instance  has estimated that the campaign there  which focused on getting elderly the  shots first  that it saved over 30 000  hospitalizations  and and 10 000 lives so far  but that's also left a large segment of  the population  younger people um unvaccinated for  for many months and of course as the  group  that's most regularly exposed they're  now fueling  a new outbreak there so what's the best  prioritization strategy i don't really  know if you can put it that way i'd say  that there are just strategies  with different outcomes

